B.Mi=new A.bh(B.v6,['# The Lost Kitten\nkind: story\nauthor: Penny Park\nillustrator: Milo Reyes\ncover: A small gray kitten with white whiskers peeking out of a green hedge\n\n## Contents\n1. The Empty Bowl | 2\n2. Looking Up and Down | 4\n3. A Tiny Meow | 6\n4. Home Again | 8\n\n## Page 2 | ch1\nMia filled the bowl with kitten food. "Here, Pepper!" she called. Pepper was a small gray kitten with white whiskers. But Pepper did not come. The bowl stayed full.\n\n## Page 3 | ch1\nMia looked under the bed. She looked behind the couch. "Mom, I can\'t find Pepper!" said Mia. "Let\'s search the whole house," said Mom.\n\n## Page 4 | ch2\nThey looked in the closet. They looked in the laundry basket. There was no Pepper anywhere. Then Mia saw that the back door was open a little. "Oh no," she whispered. "Pepper went outside!"\n\n## Page 5 | ch2\nMia ran into the yard. Her neighbor, Mr. Lee, was watering his flowers. "Have you seen a gray kitten?" asked Mia. "I have not," said Mr. Lee, "but I will help you search."\n\n## Page 6 | ch3\nMia and Mr. Lee walked past the tall hedge. They stopped and listened. "Meow," said a tiny voice. It came from inside the hedge!\n\n## Page 7 | ch3\nMia peeked between the leaves. There sat Pepper. The wind was cold, and the little kitten began to shiver. "Don\'t be scared," said Mia softly. "I am here."\n\n## Page 8 | ch4\nMr. Lee reached in and gently lifted Pepper out. "That was a quick rescue!" he said. Mia gave the kitten a big hug.\n\n## Page 9 | ch4\nAt home, Mia wrapped Pepper in a snug blanket. "No more going outside for you," she said with a smile. Pepper ate every bite from her bowl.\n\n## Glossary\nhedge: a row of bushes growing close together\nrescue: to save someone from danger\nsearch: to look carefully for something\nshiver: to shake a little because you are cold\nsnug: warm and cozy\nwhisker: a long, stiff hair on a cat\'s face\n\n## Index\nback door: 4\nblanket: 9\nhedge: 6\nMia: 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9\nMr. Lee: 5, 6, 8\nPepper: 2, 3, 4, 7, 8, 9\n','# Zoe\'s Garden\nkind: story\nauthor: Hannah Birch\nillustrator: Theo Marsh\ncover: A smiling girl in a straw hat holding a watering can beside a tiny green sprout\n\n## Contents\n1. A Tiny Seed | 2\n2. Water and Wait | 4\n3. The First Sprout | 6\n4. Flowers for Everyone | 8\n\n## Page 2 | ch1\nGrandpa gave Zoe a small paper bag. "What is in it?" asked Zoe. "Open it and see," said Grandpa. Inside was one tiny seed. It was round and brown, like a speck of dust.\n\n## Page 3 | ch1\nZoe and Grandpa filled a pot with soil. Zoe made a little hole with her finger. She dropped in the seed and covered it up. "Now it can sleep," said Grandpa.\n\n## Page 4 | ch2\nEvery morning, Zoe gave the pot a drink of water. Every afternoon, she moved it into the sun. Nothing happened on Monday. Nothing happened on Tuesday.\n\n## Page 5 | ch2\n"Is my seed broken?" Zoe asked. "No," said Grandpa. "Growing takes time. You have to be patient." Zoe sighed. Waiting was hard.\n\n## Page 6 | ch3\nOn Friday, Zoe ran to the window. Something green was poking out of the soil! "Grandpa, come quick!" she shouted. "My seed has a sprout!"\n\n## Page 7 | ch3\nThe sprout grew taller each day. Soon it had two leaves, and then four. Zoe measured it with a ruler. "It is as tall as my thumb," she said proudly.\n\n## Page 8 | ch4\nOne sunny morning, a yellow bloom opened at the top of the plant. It had soft petals that felt like velvet. "It is the prettiest flower in the world," whispered Zoe.\n\n## Page 9 | ch4\nZoe picked a seed from the old flower and put it in a bag. She gave it to her friend Sam. "Open it and see," she said with a grin.\n\n## Glossary\nbloom: a flower that has opened\npatient: able to wait without getting upset\npetal: one soft, colored part of a flower\nseed: a tiny thing that can grow into a new plant\nsoil: the dirt that plants grow in\nsprout: a new, small plant that has just started to grow\n\n## Index\nGrandpa: 2, 3, 5, 6\nleaves: 7\nSam: 9\nsun: 4\nwater: 4\nZoe: 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9\n','# The Big Bake Sale\nkind: story\nauthor: Carla Finch\nillustrator: Ben Ortiz\ncover: Three children behind a table piled with cupcakes and cookies, with a colorful banner\n\n## Contents\n1. The Plan | 2\n2. Mix and Bake | 4\n3. Setting Up | 6\n4. Sold Out | 8\n\n## Page 2 | ch1\n"Our school needs new books for the library," said Mrs. Gray. "Who has an idea?" Eli raised his hand. "We can have a bake sale!" he said. Everyone cheered.\n\n## Page 3 | ch1\nAfter class, Eli, Ana, and Max made a list. They would bring cupcakes, cookies, and lemon bars. "I will make a sign," said Ana. "I will count the coins," said Max.\n\n## Page 4 | ch2\nThat night, Eli read the recipe out loud. "Two cups of flour, one cup of sugar, and two eggs." Mom helped him stir the batter until it was smooth.\n\n## Page 5 | ch2\nEli poured the batter into a pan. Mom put it in the oven. Soon the whole kitchen smelled sweet. "Is it ready yet?" asked Eli. "Not yet," said Mom. "Be patient."\n\n## Page 6 | ch3\nOn Saturday, the friends set up a table outside the school. Ana hung her sign. It said, "Bake Sale! Cookies 50 cents." Max set out a jar for the coins.\n\n## Page 7 | ch3\nThe first customer was Mr. Diaz. "I will take two cookies, please," he said. Max counted out the change. Soon a long line of people waited at the table.\n\n## Page 8 | ch4\nBy noon, every cupcake was gone. Every cookie was gone too. "We are sold out!" shouted Ana. The three friends danced around the table.\n\n## Page 9 | ch4\nMax counted the coins one more time. "We made forty dollars!" he said. On Monday, Mrs. Gray ordered new books for the library. "Thank you, bakers," she said.\n\n## Glossary\nbatter: a wet mix of flour, eggs, and milk that is baked into a cake or cookie\nchange: the money you get back when you pay with too much\ncoin: a flat, round piece of metal money\ncustomer: a person who buys something\nrecipe: a list of what to put in food and how to make it\nsign: a board with words or pictures that tells people something\n\n## Index\nAna: 3, 6, 8\ncupcakes: 3\nEli: 2, 3, 4, 5\nlibrary: 2, 9\nMax: 3, 6, 7, 9\nMrs. Gray: 2, 9\n',"# Animal Homes\nkind: informational\nauthor: Lena Wood\nillustrator: Sam Pike\ncover: A cross-section picture showing a rabbit burrow, a bird nest in a tree, and a beaver lodge in a pond\n\n## Contents\n1. Homes Everywhere | 2\n2. Under the Ground | 4\n3. Up High | 6\n4. By the Water | 8\n\n## Page 2 | ch1\nEvery animal needs a home. A home keeps an animal safe from danger. It also keeps the animal warm and dry. The place where an animal lives is called its habitat.\n\n## Page 3 | ch1\nAnimals live in many different places. Some live in forests. Some live in deserts. Some live in the ocean. Each animal has a home that fits its needs.\n\n## Page 4 | ch2\nRabbits dig a burrow. A burrow is a hole under the ground. It has many tunnels and rooms. A rabbit sleeps there and hides from danger.\n\n## Page 5 | ch2\nA fox sleeps in a den. A den may be a hollow log or a hole in a hillside. Mother foxes raise their pups in the den. Moles also live under the ground and dig long tunnels.\n\n## Page 6 | ch3\nBirds build a nest. A nest is made from twigs, grass, and feathers. The mother bird lays eggs in the nest. The nest is high in a tree, out of reach.\n\n## Page 7 | ch3\nBees live in a hive. A hive is made of wax. Thousands of bees live and work together in one hive. They store sweet honey there.\n\n## Page 8 | ch4\nBeavers build a lodge. A lodge is a home made of sticks and mud. It sits in the middle of a pond. Beavers swim in underwater doors to get inside.\n\n## Page 9 | ch4\nBeavers also build a dam. A dam is a wall of sticks that holds back water. Behind the dam, a deep pond fills up. The pond is a safe habitat for the beavers.\n\n## Glossary\nburrow: a hole in the ground where an animal lives\nden: a hidden home of a wild animal, like a fox\nhabitat: the place where an animal lives\nhive: a home where bees live and make honey\nlodge: a beaver's home made of sticks and mud\nnest: a home that birds make from twigs, grass, and feathers\n\n## Index\nbeavers: 8, 9\nbees: 7\nbirds: 6\ndam: 9\neggs: 6\nfoxes: 5\nhoney: 7\nmoles: 5\nrabbits: 4\n","# Weather Watchers\nkind: informational\nauthor: Jules Hart\nillustrator: Nina Cole\ncover: Two children in raincoats holding a weather chart, with a sun, a cloud, and a rainbow behind them\n\n## Contents\n1. Sun and Wind | 2\n2. Clouds and Rain | 4\n3. Thunder and Lightning | 6\n4. Snow and Ice | 8\n\n## Page 2 | ch1\nWeather is what the sky and air are like outside. Is it sunny? Is it windy? The sun warms the air. The temperature tells us how warm or cold it is.\n\n## Page 3 | ch1\nWind is air that moves. A light wind can blow a kite high in the sky. A strong wind can bend tall trees. We use a windsock to see which way the wind blows.\n\n## Page 4 | ch2\nClouds are made of tiny drops of water. When many drops join together, they get heavy. Then they fall to the ground as rain.\n\n## Page 5 | ch2\nRain gives plants and animals water to drink. Puddles form on the ground. After a storm, you might see a rainbow. A forecast tells us when rain may come.\n\n## Page 6 | ch3\nA big storm can make thunder. Thunder is a loud rumbling sound. It comes after lightning. Lightning is a bright flash of electricity in the sky.\n\n## Page 7 | ch3\nYou see lightning before you hear thunder because light travels faster than sound. When you hear thunder, go inside. A building is the safest place to be.\n\n## Page 8 | ch4\nWhen the temperature drops very low, water can freeze. Rain turns into ice. Clouds can make snow. Snowflakes are tiny ice crystals that fall softly to the ground.\n\n## Page 9 | ch4\nNo two snowflakes are the same. Each one has its own shape. Snow can cover a whole town. Weather watchers keep warm with coats, hats, and mittens.\n\n## Glossary\nforecast: a report that says what the weather will be\nfreeze: to turn into ice because it is very cold\nlightning: a bright flash of electricity in the sky\ntemperature: how hot or cold something is\nthunder: the loud sound that comes after lightning\nweather: what the sky and air are like outside\n\n## Index\nclouds: 4, 8\nice: 8\nkite: 3\nlightning: 6, 7\nrain: 4, 5, 8\nrainbow: 5\nsnowflakes: 8, 9\nsun: 2\nthunder: 6, 7\nwind: 3\n","# Dinosaur Detectives\nkind: informational\nauthor: Max Larkin\nillustrator: Ivy Tran\ncover: A scientist with a small brush kneeling beside a huge dinosaur bone in a sandy canyon\n\n## Contents\n1. Bones in the Ground | 2\n2. Careful Digging | 4\n3. Putting It Together | 6\n4. What We Learn | 8\n\n## Page 2 | ch1\nDinosaurs lived millions of years ago. They are extinct now, so no dinosaurs are alive today. But we know about them because of fossils.\n\n## Page 3 | ch1\nA fossil is what is left of a plant or animal from long ago. When a dinosaur died, mud and sand covered its bones. Over a very long time, the bones turned into stone.\n\n## Page 4 | ch2\nA paleontologist is a scientist who studies fossils. Paleontologists search in deserts and canyons. They look for bones that stick out of the rock.\n\n## Page 5 | ch2\nPaleontologists dig very slowly. They use small brushes and tiny tools. One wrong move could break a fossil. They write notes and take photos of every bone they find.\n\n## Page 6 | ch3\nThe bones are wrapped in plaster to keep them safe. Then a truck carries them to a museum. A museum is a place where people can see special things.\n\n## Page 7 | ch3\nAt the museum, workers clean the bones. Then they fit the pieces together like a puzzle. The pieces make a skeleton. A skeleton is all of the bones of an animal.\n\n## Page 8 | ch4\nA dinosaur's teeth show what it ate. Sharp teeth were for eating meat. Flat teeth were for chewing leaves. The size of the bones shows how big the dinosaur was.\n\n## Page 9 | ch4\nFootprints show how dinosaurs walked. Some fossils are eggs. Every fossil is a clue. Dinosaur detectives are still digging and still learning.\n\n## Glossary\nextinct: no longer living anywhere on Earth\nfossil: the remains of a plant or animal from long ago, turned to stone\nmuseum: a place where people go to see and learn about special things\npaleontologist: a scientist who studies fossils\nplaster: a soft mix that hardens to protect something\nskeleton: all of the bones of an animal\n\n## Index\nbrushes: 5\neggs: 9\nfootprints: 9\nmud: 3\npuzzle: 7\nteeth: 8\n",'# The Rainy-Day Campout\nkind: story\nauthor: Tara Quill\nillustrator: Gus Bellamy\ncover: A blanket tent with a glowing flashlight inside and raindrops on the window behind it\n\n## Contents\n1. Rain, Rain, Go Away | 2\n2. Building the Tent | 4\n3. Stories by Flashlight | 6\n4. A Surprise at Bedtime | 8\n\n## Page 2 | ch1\nRain tapped on the windows all morning. Nora and Leo had planned to camp in the backyard, but the grass was soaked. "No campout today," said Nora with a sigh. Leo kicked off his slippers. "It is not fair!" he shouted.\n\n## Page 3 | ch1\nDad looked up from his newspaper. "Who says we cannot camp?" he asked. "We can have a campout right here in the living room." Nora and Leo looked at each other. Then they grinned from ear to ear.\n\n## Page 4 | ch2\nFirst they needed a tent. Leo dragged two chairs across the room. Nora found the biggest blanket in the whole house. "Hold this corner," said Dad. "I will tie the other end." Soon a wide, wobbly tent stood beside the sofa.\n\n## Page 5 | ch2\nInside, the tent felt cozy and dark. Nora spread soft pillows on the floor. Leo piled a cushion in every corner. "Now it looks like a real campsite," Nora whispered. "All we need is a campfire," Leo said.\n\n## Page 6 | ch3\nDad brought three flashlights and a bag of popcorn. He switched off the lights. Thunder rumbled outside, and Leo jumped. "Was that the storm?" he asked in a tiny voice. "It cannot get us in here," said Nora.\n\n## Page 7 | ch3\nDad held a flashlight under his chin and made his face look spooky. His shadow stretched across the ceiling. "Once upon a time," he said, "a dragon lived in a cave." Nora clapped. "Is it a friendly dragon?" she asked.\n\n## Page 8 | ch4\nBy bedtime the rain had stopped. Mom peeked into the tent. "Time to brush your teeth," she said. "Oh, Mom, please!" cried Leo. "Can we sleep in the tent tonight?" Mom smiled and looked at Dad. "Yes," she said, "but only if you stay quiet."\n\n## Page 9 | ch4\nSo Nora and Leo brushed their teeth and crawled back into the tent. The flashlights clicked off one by one. Far away, thunder grumbled once more. "Good night, Leo," Nora whispered. "Good night, Nora," Leo said sleepily. Outside, the last raindrops danced on the window.\n\n## Glossary\ncozy: warm, soft and comfortable\ncushion: a soft pillow to sit or lean on\nflashlight: a small light you hold in your hand\nshadow: a dark shape made when something blocks the light\ntent: a shelter made of cloth that you can sleep in\nthunder: the loud rumbling sound you hear during a storm\n\n## Index\nblanket: 4\ncampout: 2, 3\nDad: 3, 4, 6, 7, 8\nMom: 8\npopcorn: 6\nrain: 2, 8\nthunder: 6, 9\n','# Robot Joe\'s First Day\nkind: story\nauthor: Felix Marlow\nillustrator: Dina Cho\ncover: A small silver robot with a red backpack holding a big yellow pencil\n\n## Contents\n1. A New Student | 2\n2. Too Many Rules | 4\n3. The Messy Paint | 6\n4. Friends at Last | 8\n\n## Page 2 | ch1\nOn Monday morning, a new student rolled into Room 5. He was a small silver robot with a red backpack. "Good morning, class," said Ms. Reed. "This is Joe. Today is his first day of school." The children stared. Joe\'s gears gave a happy whir.\n\n## Page 3 | ch1\nA girl named Tess raised her hand. "Can robots do math?" she asked. "Of course," Joe replied. "I can add, subtract, and count to a million." Omar giggled. "That would take forever," he said.\n\n## Page 4 | ch2\nJoe wanted to follow every rule. He raised his hand before he spoke. He pushed in his chair and walked on the right side of the hall. "Rule one: be polite," Joe said to Tess. "Rule two: be tidy." Tess laughed. "You do not need a rule for everything," she said.\n\n## Page 5 | ch2\nAt lunch, Joe watched the children share their crackers. "Why do you give away your food?" he asked. "Because friends share," said Omar. Joe\'s gears went click-click. "I do not eat lunch," he said. "I run on batteries." Omar handed him an extra apple anyway.\n\n## Page 6 | ch3\nIn art class, Ms. Reed gave everyone a brush and a jar of paint. Joe held his brush very carefully. Then his arm began to wobble, and red paint splashed across the table. "Oh no!" he cried. "I broke the tidy rule!" The whole class went quiet.\n\n## Page 7 | ch3\nTess grabbed a paper towel. "Do not worry," she said. "Messes are part of art." Omar dipped his thumb in the paint and stamped a red dot on his nose. "Look, I am a clown!" he shouted. Even Joe let out a loud, beepy laugh.\n\n## Page 8 | ch4\nAfter school, the children decided to invent a game called Robot Tag. Joe could not be caught because he rolled so fast. "You are too quick!" called Tess. "We need a new rule," said Omar. "Everyone gets a turn to be fast." Joe beamed. "I like that rule," he said.\n\n## Page 9 | ch4\nThat night, Joe plugged himself in and thought about his day. He had learned a rule that was better than all the others. "Be kind," he said softly to the moon. Then his gears slowed, and the little robot fell asleep.\n\n## Glossary\ngear: a wheel with teeth that helps a machine move\ninvent: to think up and make something new\npolite: having good manners\nrule: a statement that tells you what to do or not to do\ntidy: neat and in order\nwobble: to shake or move from side to side\n\n## Index\napple: 5\nbackpack: 2\ncrackers: 5\nMs. Reed: 2, 6\nOmar: 3, 5, 7, 8\npaint: 6, 7\nTess: 3, 4, 7, 8\n','# The Missing Lunchbox\nkind: story\nauthor: Ruth Kessler\nillustrator: Carlos Pym\ncover: An open red lunchbox on a school desk with a magnifying glass beside it\n\n## Contents\n1. Where Is It? | 2\n2. Following the Clues | 4\n3. The Wrong Suspect | 6\n4. Case Closed | 8\n\n## Page 2 | ch1\nAt lunchtime, Ben opened his backpack and froze. His red lunchbox was gone! He checked under the desk and behind the coat hooks. "My lunchbox is missing," he said. "It was here this morning." Lila gasped. "This is a mystery!" she said.\n\n## Page 3 | ch1\nLila pulled a notebook from her bag. "We should be detectives," she said. "Detectives look for clues." Ben looked puzzled. "What is a clue?" he asked. "A clue is a small hint that helps solve a mystery," said Lila.\n\n## Page 4 | ch2\nFirst, the detectives studied the floor. Lila knelt down and pointed at the tiles. "Notice these crumbs?" she whispered. "They lead toward the door." Ben followed the trail of cracker crumbs out into the hall.\n\n## Page 5 | ch2\nThe crumbs stopped near the gym door. Mr. Park was mopping the hall. "Have you seen a red lunchbox?" Ben asked. Mr. Park rubbed his chin. "I saw Theo run past here," he said. "He was in a big hurry."\n\n## Page 6 | ch3\nBen and Lila found Theo by the water fountain. He was munching crackers from a plastic bag. "Did you take my lunchbox?" Ben asked. Theo\'s eyes grew wide. "No way!" he said. "I only brought these from home." Lila wrote his name on her list of suspects, and then crossed it out.\n\n## Page 7 | ch3\nBack in the classroom, Ben slumped into his chair. "I will never find it," he whispered. Lila put a hand on his shoulder. "Detectives never give up," she said. "Think. Where were you last?"\n\n## Page 8 | ch4\nBen closed his eyes and thought hard. Gym class! He remembered stuffing something into his blue gym bag. "I think I know!" he shouted. The friends raced toward the gym. "Walk, please!" called Mrs. Cole.\n\n## Page 9 | ch4\nInside the blue gym bag, Ben found a red lunchbox. A sandwich, an apple, and two cookies sat inside, just as he had packed them. "I put it in the wrong bag!" he said with a groan. Lila laughed. "Mystery solved," she said. "Case closed, Detective Ben."\n\n## Glossary\nclue: a small hint that helps you solve a mystery\ndetective: a person who looks for clues to solve a mystery\nmystery: something strange that has no answer yet\nnotice: to see something and pay attention to it\npuzzled: confused and not sure what is going on\nsuspect: a person you think may have done something\n\n## Index\ncrumbs: 4, 5\ngym: 5, 8, 9\nlunchbox: 2, 5, 6, 9\nMr. Park: 5\nMrs. Cole: 8\nnotebook: 3\nTheo: 5, 6\n','# Penny Penguin\'s Big Jump\nkind: story\nauthor: Orla Sand\nillustrator: Wes Tamura\ncover: A small penguin at the edge of an ice cliff above sparkling blue water\n\n## Contents\n1. The Edge of the Ice | 2\n2. Practice Makes Splash | 4\n3. A Cold, Windy Day | 6\n4. The Big Jump | 8\n\n## Page 2 | ch1\nPenny the penguin stood at the edge of the tall ice cliff. Far below, the blue water sparkled. All of her friends had already jumped. "Come on, Penny!" called Pip from the water. "It is easy!" Penny\'s flippers shook. "It looks very far," she whispered.\n\n## Page 3 | ch1\nGran waddled up beside her. She wore a knitted scarf and a warm smile. "Are you scared?" she asked. "A little," said Penny. "Everyone is scared sometimes," said Gran. "Being brave does not mean you feel no fear."\n\n## Page 4 | ch2\nSo Penny decided to practice. She started on a small ice step near the shore. Finn the seal floated nearby, watching. "Bend your knees," he called. "Then push off with your flippers." Penny hopped, and her belly landed in a puddle with a loud splash. "Again!" called Finn.\n\n## Page 5 | ch2\nPenny tried again and again. Her jumps grew higher each time. By afternoon she could leap off the highest step. "I did it!" she cheered. Pip swam over to the shore. "You are ready," he said. "Tomorrow you can jump from the cliff!"\n\n## Page 6 | ch3\nBut the next morning, a cold wind howled across the glacier. Gray clouds hid the sun. The air felt chilly, and the cliff looked taller than ever. "I cannot do it," said Penny. "The wind is too strong." "Let us wait inside," said Gran.\n\n## Page 7 | ch3\nThey sat in a snowy cave and shared warm fish soup. "When I was young," Gran said, "I was the last penguin to jump. I stood at that cliff for a whole hour." Penny\'s eyes grew round. "What happened?" she asked. "I jumped," said Gran, "and it was the best day of my life."\n\n## Page 8 | ch4\nBy afternoon the wind had calmed. Penny walked back to the cliff with Gran and Pip beside her. The water shimmered far below. Gran squeezed her flipper. "We are right here," said Gran. "One, two, three!" called Pip. Penny took a deep breath. "I am brave," she whispered.\n\n## Page 9 | ch4\nPenny leaped. For one long moment she flew through the cold air with her flippers spread wide. Then she hit the water with a giant splash. "Hooray!" yelled Pip. "You did it!" Penny popped up laughing. "Let us do it again!" she shouted.\n\n## Glossary\nbrave: ready to face something scary\nchilly: cold enough to feel uncomfortable\ncliff: a high, steep wall of rock or ice\nflipper: a flat limb that a penguin uses to swim\nglacier: a huge, slow river of ice\nsplash: the sound and spray when something hits water\n\n## Index\ncliff: 2, 5, 6, 7, 8\nFinn: 4\nGran: 3, 6, 7, 8\nPip: 2, 5, 8, 9\nsoup: 7\nwater: 2, 8, 9\nwind: 6, 8\n'],t.li)
